WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.872
Hannah: Describe your tests for crash recovery. Which failure windows would you deliberately exercise?

2
00:00:07.872 --> 00:00:16.128
Raj: I'd use fault injection around each transition. Just to confirm, we can simulate lost responses, right?

3
00:00:16.128 --> 00:00:23.567
Lukas: Yes, include success before local persistence. Then crash before queue acknowledgement as well.

4
00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:31.847
Raj: Okay, each restart reads the persisted operation. Tests assert one applied effect, not one request.

5
00:00:31.847 --> 00:00:40.319
Raj: I'd dry-run it first with deterministic fakes. Maybe property tests help explore duplicate message sequences.

6
00:00:40.319 --> 00:00:47.831
Hannah: How do you test concurrent workers? A sequential duplicate test won't expose claim races.

7
00:00:47.831 --> 00:00:55.391
Raj: I'd coordinate contenders with a controlled barrier. We can rule that out only after racing claims.

8
00:00:55.391 --> 00:01:02.494
Lukas: Would you also test the real contract? Fake deduplication could be stronger than production behavior.

9
00:01:02.494 --> 00:01:11.926
Raj: Yes, a sandbox smoke test checks deduplication. One action item covers retention expiry and payload mismatch.

10
00:01:11.926 --> 00:01:20.230
Raj: I'd also test stale versions and oversized input. Those cases must avoid sending unsafe updates.

